 "narrative": {
  "northStar": "The Logbook",
  "overview": "ONS is a logbook for one person's year, and the week is the unit. Days are noisy and years are abstract; a week is the span you can steer. Home is the week itself, a strip of days across with the owner's lanes down the side (weather, running, CrossFit, habits, check-in, plans, CFB), so \"what's left this week\" is visible as empty cells. Every page opens with a plain title, the span it covers and one coach-voice paragraph, then the data.\n\nEach life area owns a colour and keeps it everywhere: running is blue, CrossFit amber, reading teal, habits green, things you write violet, plans rose, CFB tangerine, weather slate. Forest is the system's own voice: today, selection, focus. The colour is there to find things fast, not to decorate. The UI comes in light and dark, following the device until the owner picks.\n\nMost ONS surfaces are **Operate** surfaces: dashboards, slates, trackers and forms used every day. Read-heavy pages such as briefs, reviews and postmortems are **Read**. Precision, density and scanability outrank expression. The one deliberate indulgence is a small pixel-art scene beside each page title, which the owner asked for. It is the only thing on a page that moves, and it follows strict rules (see Pixel Scenes).\n\nThe current reference build is the static mockup in `docs/design/directions/6-logbook-color/` (`home.html`, `cfb.html`, `style.css`, `theme.js`, `px.js`). Until the app migrates, that `style.css` is the token source; afterwards `web/app/globals.css` and `web/tailwind.config.js` are.",
  "keyCharacteristics": [
   "A tinted L-frame (sidebar and 48px top bar) holding the work on a white sheet with 16px corners",
   "The week strip: lanes by days, today marked by forest lines, missing items as dashed outlines",
   "One colour per life area, as a tint, an ink and a mark, used the same way on every page",
   "Forest for the system's own states only: today, selection, focus, active nav",
   "Light and dark themes from one token set, switched instantly",
   "IBM Plex Sans for words, IBM Plex Mono for figures in tables and marks",
   "Flat: borders and tints, no shadows",
   "A pixel-art title scene per page, the one moving thing"
  ],
  "rules": [
   {
    "name": "The One Lane, One Colour Rule",
    "body": "An area's colour means that area, everywhere: lane label, marks, goal bar, tinted card, nav icon. Don't reuse an area hue for something unrelated. The one sanctioned overlap is violet: \"things you write\" on Home and \"favourite picks\" on the slate, which never share a page. Adding a ninth area reuses a hue or gets an explicit decision; it never gets a new hue invented on the spot.",
    "section": "colors"
   },
   {
    "name": "The Forest Is The System Rule",
    "body": "Forest marks what the system knows about you right now: today, selected, focused. Area colours never do those jobs.",
    "section": "colors"
   },
   {
    "name": "The Two Themes, One Token Rule",
    "body": "Every colour exists as a token in both themes. Never hard-code a hex in a component; a colour that only works in one theme is a bug.",
    "section": "colors"
   },
   {
    "name": "The No Micro-Caps Rule",
    "body": "Nothing is uppercase and tracked. Labels are sentence case at 12px or more.",
    "section": "typography"
   },
   {
    "name": "The Mono Counts Rule",
    "body": "Figures that line up in columns or marks are Plex Mono. Words are Plex Sans.",
    "section": "typography"
   },
   {
    "name": "The Flat Sheet Rule",
    "body": "If something needs to stand apart, give it a tint or a border. Never a shadow.",
    "section": "elevation"
   }
  ],
  "dos": [
   "Do open every page with a title, a span line and one coach-voice paragraph before any data.",
   "Do colour by life area, using the tint, ink and mark the same way on every page.",
   "Do reserve forest for today, selection, focus and the active nav.",
   "Do define every colour in both themes, and check both themes at 1440 and 390 before calling a page done.",
   "Do show missing, due, stale and \"not wired\" as designed states (dashed outlines, explicit pills), never as blanks.",
   "Do label backtest figures as backtest and keep them apart from live results.",
   "Do keep scenes to the Pixel Scenes rules, and grow a scene with things from the page's own world."
  ],
  "donts": [
   "Don't add drop shadows to anything.",
   "Don't invent a new hue for an area, or use an area colour for something unrelated.",
   "Don't use uppercase tracked labels or text under 12px.",
   "Don't animate data, keyboard actions, the slate or habit check-ins. The pixel scene is the only motion besides 120–150ms press and hover feedback.",
   "Don't scale pixel art by fractions, smooth it, or give a character a fill that matches its outline.",
   "Don't hard-code a colour that only works in one theme."
  ]
 }
